\begin{abstract}
Adapting a pretrained time series foundation model (TSFM) to a downstream task typically involves attaching a single adapter head to the frozen backbone. A natural extension is a \emph{mixture of expert adapters} with a learned router, so that different input windows can be served by different adapter topologies. We show that this natural extension \textbf{catastrophically fails} on TSFMs that use instance normalization (RevIN): routing entropy collapses to $0.000{\pm}0.000$, because RevIN strips the per-window mean and scale that the router needs for discriminative gating. We call this failure \textbf{normalization-induced routing collapse} and isolate it through seven converging causal controls---most strikingly, opposite entropy trajectories under identical unfreezing on identical data (Timer-XL rises $1.49{\to}1.60$ while MOMENT collapses $0.60{\to}0.00$)---while all eleven standard MoE-rescue mechanisms fail ($2.7{\times}$ worse; 720 runs). A mutual-information decomposition (Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}) formalizes this: normalization destroys routing MI by an amount equal to the information in the stripped statistics, and the resulting signal-ratio predictor identifies which datasets benefit ($\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{<}0.002$, $n{=}9$), including where it does \emph{not} help (Traffic, $R{=}0.14$). Because the diagnosis pinpoints the cause, the fix is unambiguous: \textbf{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters (RR-MoA)} routes on the raw, pre-normalization input while the backbone stays frozen. RR-MoA achieves \textbf{54/54 wins} ($p{<}0.001$) on the primary backbone, generalizes across four additional backbones (two negative controls confirm normalization-specificity), and produces a \textbf{Frozen Paradox} where frozen adapters beat full fine-tuning by $12$--$79\%$. A dual-stream extension, \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}}, achieves \textbf{parity-or-better with DLinear on $107/123$ cells} across six backbones ($65$ significant wins vs $11$ losses), with $15/16$ losses at $H{\geq}192$ and $100\%$ on Timer-XL and Moirai-MoE.
\end{abstract}

